  <static seed="351" source="Library/Michigan/Chap14Sec7/Q05.pg">
    <statement><p>Calculate all
four second-order partial derivatives and check that <m>f_{xy}=f_{yx}</m>.
Assume the variables are restricted to a domain on which the function
is defined.
<me>f(x,y) = e^{5xy}</me></p>
<p><m>f_{xx} =</m> <fillin characters="55" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p><m>f_{yy} =</m> <fillin characters="55" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
<p><m>f_{xy} =</m> <fillin characters="55" name="AnSwEr0003"/></p>
<p><m>f_{yx} =</m> <fillin characters="55" name="AnSwEr0004"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>25y^{2}e^{5xy}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>25x^{2}e^{5xy}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>5e^{5xy}+25xye^{5xy}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>5e^{5xy}+25xye^{5xy}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>f_x = 5ye^{5xy}</m> and <m>f_y = 5xe^{5xy}</m>, so
<me>f_{xx} = 25y^{2}e^{5xy}, f_{yy} = 25x^{2}e^{5xy},</me>
and
<me>f_{xy} = 5e^{5xy}+25xye^{5xy} = f_{yx} = 5e^{5xy}+25xye^{5xy}.</me>



</solution>
